# Inertia.js + React Frontend Conventions

## File Organization

- Pages: `resources/js/Pages/{Module}/Index.tsx` + `{Feature}Form.tsx`
- Components: `resources/js/Components/{Domain}/{Specific}/ComponentName.tsx`
- Form inputs: `resources/js/Components/Forms/Inputs/{ComponentName}/`
- Hooks: `resources/js/hooks/use{Purpose}.ts` (e.g., useJobStatus, useNotificationSound)
- Utils: `resources/js/lib/` for shared utilities

## TypeScript

- Strict mode enabled
- Use `interface {Component}Props` for all component props
- Export interfaces when reusable across components
- Proper typing everywhere — no `any` unless absolutely unavoidable

## Inertia.js Usage

- Take full advantage of Inertia tools: `useForm`, `Link`, `router`
- Use `useForm()` from `@inertiajs/react` for all form handling
- Use `router` for programmatic navigation
- Use `usePage()` for shared data access
- All API calls optimized for InertiaJS
- Use axios over Inertia only when it genuinely makes more sense (background calls, non-navigation requests, polling)

## Component Patterns

- Functional components only — no class components
- Separate logic from markup as much as possible, but don't over-fragment small code into excessive files
- Event handlers with "handle" prefix: `handleClick`, `handleKeyDown`, `handleSubmit`
- Keep files small — extract reusable logic into hooks or utils
- Fully responsive components

## Code Quality

- Error handling with try-catch blocks and proper logging
- Use early returns for readability
- Descriptive variable names always (never abbreviations)
- Check for existing reusable components before creating new ones
